WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:14.120
When you want to watch a movie or a TV show,
you know what to do.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.490
You tap your fingers and within a few seconds
you are watching whatever you want.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:20.029
It's frictionless. It's easy.

00:00:20.029 --> 00:00:24.020
But in some places, it's not so easy.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:30.200
"There's no other way we don't have foreign
TV, it's national TV."

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.010
Officially, there is one source of permitted media in
Cuba. And it's the state.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:42.580
"It's because I don't know what to tell you
tell you brother. I just know that's the way it is."

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:43.530
 

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.100
But Cubans found a way to work around this media blockade

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.200
to distribute the latest in global entertainment to the entire country,

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.200
coordinated entirely by an informal market of underground data traffickers,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.200
based both in Havana and in the United States.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.500
It's the fruits of the internet, without any
of the guts.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:14.830
"One guy will gather TV shows. The other Movies.
Music etc. and that's how they go about collecting

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.670
media until they have 1 terabyte."

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:22.490
Once all the latest media from around the
world is gathered onto one hard drive, the top dealers

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.800
in Havana will prepare it for distribution.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.700
This extensive collection of digital media is affectionately called 'El Paquete Semanal' -- The Weekly Package.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.729
"Someone brings it to me. It's from someone
who gets it from someone else who gets it

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:44.300
from someone else.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.930
In the end it's a chain. It costs about $2
and what most people do is fill their flashdrive

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:52.689
with the content they want."

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:57.400
A quick look into the Paquete, and you realize
just how massive the system must be to gather

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.780
so much content in such a short of time

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:06.400
especially in a country that has 5% internet
penetration and agonizingly slow connection

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:56.639
speeds.

00:02:56.639 --> 00:03:03.290
Who's behind all this?

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:10.859
I found a couple of 20 year olds who make
their living hustling Havana's streets, participating

00:03:10.859 --> 00:03:14.079
in the thriving industry of shadowy odd jobs
.

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:18.760
Between driving taxi bikes without a license
and giving unofficial city tours to European

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.980
tourists, these guys move The Paqeute between
distributors from time to time.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.620
They led me behind the scenes in Havana where
I saw how the Paquete makes its rounds every

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.540
week.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:34.029
The distribution centers are usually in the
back of a cell phone repair shop or a DVD

00:03:34.029 --> 00:03:34.849
store.

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:38.349
Hardware flowing in and out of these places
doesn't attract unwanted attention.

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:43.939
Distributors were not keen on appearing on
camera, but they showed me their operation

00:03:43.939 --> 00:03:49.079
and explained how clients come to their shop
with a weekly order of content.

00:03:49.079 --> 00:03:53.839
American TV shows are some of the most demanded
content, but some people show up with an empty

00:03:53.839 --> 00:03:57.040
drive every week to transfer the whole thing

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:03.519
everything from the iPhone app updates to
the indie film section.

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:07.949
After poking around, I finally worked my way
up the chain to discover who is coordinating

00:04:07.949 --> 00:04:09.209
this massive enterprise.

00:04:09.209 --> 00:04:31.120
They call him Dany Paquete, and he agreed
to meet with me.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.620
Dany was with a client so I waited for an
hour in his impromptu lobby.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:44.540
I didn't know what to expect Dany Paquete
to be like. But i definitely did not expect

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:44.960
this.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.060
He's a 26 year old who was wearing board shorts
and not shirt when I arrived.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:55.870
He looked a lot more like an lazy college
sophomore than the king pin of a national

00:04:55.870 --> 00:05:04.310
black market of pirated media.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:08.290
But after five minutes sitting in his office
with a client in the room, I quickly gained

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:11.430
a respect for Dany and the media machine he
is running.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:16.120
As Dany's 40 year old assistant helped load
the hard drive of a local distributor, I watched

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.840
a live feed of Telemundo digitizing on the
screen.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.900
Dany must have an illegal satellite hidden
in a fake water tower on his roof, a fairly

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:26.750
common Cuban practice.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:56.900
Dany's girl friend wandered in and began watching
a discovery channel documentary about a farmer

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.200
who almost died in a tractor accident in Iowa.

